```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 6, fig.height = 4,
                      message = FALSE)
```

The multinomial logit's independence of irrelevant alternatives (IIA) is a
testable restriction, and the tests of it have a troubled history: the most
cited one, in its classic form, often yields a covariance difference that is
not positive semidefinite and a negative statistic, and implementations of the
others differ in details that change the answer (which half of the sample is
used, which weights, which covariance). `polychoice` implements the existing tests with
their definitions fixed in the package's design document, checks them by
simulation, and reports what the simulation found. This vignette walks through
the battery on the Pennsylvania vote data and then through the further
specification tests.

```{r data}
library(polychoice)
data(pa2006)
m <- mnl(vote ~ canvass + phone + democrat + republican + age + female + voted2004,
         data = pa2006)
```

## The IIA battery

### Hausman and McFadden (1984)

Under IIA, dropping an alternative and refitting on the remaining cases leaves
the common coefficients consistent, so the full-sample and restricted
estimates should agree. The classic statistic uses the *difference* of the two
covariance matrices,
\(q = (\hat\beta_r - \hat\beta_f)'(V_r - V_f)^{-1}(\hat\beta_r - \hat\beta_f)\),
which need not be positive semidefinite in finite samples; a negative
statistic is reported with a p-value of 1 and a flag. The *joint* form
estimates the covariance of \(\hat\beta_r - \hat\beta_f\) from the stacked
scores of the two fits (a seemingly-unrelated-estimation sandwich), which is
always positive semidefinite. Dropping the base alternative re-bases the model
exactly, so every alternative can be dropped.

```{r hm}
hausman_mcfadden(m)
hausman_mcfadden(m, vcov = "joint")
```

### Small and Hsiao (1985)

The sample is split in half. The full model is fit on both halves and the
two estimates are combined with the weights \(\sqrt{1/2}\) and
\(1 - \sqrt{1/2}\); the model without the dropped alternative is fit on the
second half; the statistic is minus twice the difference between the
second-half restricted log-likelihood evaluated at the combined estimates and
at its own maximum. It depends on the split, so `splits` repeats it over
several random splits and `seed` makes the result reproducible.

```{r sh}
small_hsiao(m, splits = 10, seed = 1)
```

The split matters, and on these data for a reason that has nothing to do
with IIA. Only two of the 125 nonresponders did not vote in 2004. In four of
the ten splits (1, 4, 5, and 10) both fall in the second half, so in the
first half the nonresponse equation separates on `voted2004` (its coefficient
diverges); the combined estimate then gives those two respondents a
near-zero probability in the second half, and the statistics of the three
drops that keep nonresponse exceed 50 on 16 degrees of freedom, while the
nonresponse drop, which removes that equation, stays below 26. The
`separated` column marks every row whose statistic uses a diverging
half-sample fit: the three drops that keep nonresponse in those four splits
and in split 6 (where both fall in the first half; there the separated half
carries the smaller weight and the evaluation half has neither case, so those
statistics are small), but not the nonresponse drops, which remove the
separated equation. In the five splits without separation only two of the
20 statistics reject at the 5 percent level, both in split 2. A single split
can therefore suggest a decisive rejection or none, and which one is luck: on
these data a split drawn with `small_hsiao(m, seed = s)` is separated for 12
of the seeds 1 to 20. The test is the split fixed before the data are seen
(`small_hsiao(m, seed = )`): report it, and when it is separated, as split 1
is here, report it as uninformative or calibrate it by the parametric
bootstrap with the same seed. A seed chosen after seeing a result is not a
test. The splits and their summary (the median, the share of splits
rejecting, and the share separated) describe how much the statistic varies;
when many splits separate, the test has little to say about IIA on data like
these. The summary's `median_p` is the chi-square p-value of the median
statistic, a descriptive figure: the median of several split statistics is not
itself chi-square distributed (in the package's size study a single split
had nominal size, while the battery's median rule never rejected under the
null: it is conservative by construction). When the
decision matters, calibrate the median by the parametric bootstrap: give
`boot_pvalue()` a function that returns the median statistic as a one-row
data frame, with a fixed seed so that every replicate draws its splits from
the same random stream (they are stratified on each replicate's simulated
outcome; slow: ten splits refit the model thirty times per replicate, so this
is not run here).

```{r sh-boot, eval = FALSE}
sh_median <- function(object, drop) {
  s <- attr(small_hsiao(object, drop = drop, splits = 10, seed = 1), "summary")
  data.frame(test = "Small-Hsiao median", statistic = s$median_statistic)
}
boot_pvalue(m, sh_median, B = 99, drop = "abstain")
```

### Fok and Paap (2025)

Fok and Paap (2025) take the pairwise conditional likelihoods of the
multinomial logit and build two tests: \(Q_1\), a Hausman contrast between the
maximum-likelihood and the composite-likelihood estimates (the latter with a
Godambe sandwich covariance), and \(Q_2\), a GMM overidentification test on the
stacked pairwise scores. \(Q_2\) is computed in the two-step form by default
(`gmm = "iterated"` and the projected C-statistic form are available); with
the pairs that involve the base alternative only and case-specific covariates
alone there is no overidentification and only \(Q_1\) is reported. Under
the multinomial logit the composite-likelihood estimator is nearly as
efficient as the maximum-likelihood one, so the difference of their two
separately estimated covariance matrices, which Fok and Paap's \(Q_1\) uses,
is a small difference of noisy matrices and is indefinite in nearly every
sample (the package's simulations found 44 percent of these statistics
negative). `fp_tests()` therefore estimates the covariance of the contrast
directly from the two estimators' stacked influence functions by default,
as the joint Hausman-McFadden form does; `vcov = "difference"` gives Fok and
Paap's formula.

```{r fp}
fp_tests(m)
```

### The battery

`iia_tests()` runs all of the above with one call, with ten Small-Hsiao splits
reported by their median (the row's p-value is the chi-square p-value of the
median statistic, a descriptive figure).

```{r battery}
iia_tests(m, seed = 1)
```

How to read this table for these data: the joint Hausman-McFadden rows do
not reject at the 5 percent level (the abstention row only narrowly), and
both Fok-Paap tests do. The Small-Hsiao medians are descriptive (the note
under the table), and in three of their rows half the splits are separated. The
difference-form Hausman-McFadden rows rest on covariance differences that are
not positive semidefinite (`psd = FALSE`); one rule covers every such row: a
negative statistic is no evidence against IIA (its p-value is 1), and a
positive one is unreliable, because the difference form over-rejects (next
section), so those rows are descriptive. The tests look at different
departures, so disagreement is informative rather than contradictory: the
Fok-Paap tests use all pairwise comparisons at once, while the drop tests
look at one alternative at a time (and the Small-Hsiao medians here rest
partly on separated splits). A reasonable report states the
battery, notes which rows had an indefinite covariance difference, and, when
the decision matters, calibrates the decisive statistic by the parametric
bootstrap (next section) or fits the alternative model directly (nested logit,
HEV, random intercepts; the *Beyond IIA* vignette) and compares by likelihood
ratio.

### What the size study found

The package's verification ledger records a 300-replication study of the
battery on data generated from the multinomial logit (three alternatives,
four covariates, ten parameters, 1000 cases; Monte Carlo standard error about
0.013), with these rejection rates at the nominal 5 percent level: the
Hausman-McFadden difference form was not positive semidefinite in 79 percent
of replications and rejected 8 percent of the time for a non-base drop and 16
percent for the base drop; its joint form 2 and 5 percent; Small-Hsiao (one
split) 4 and 5 percent, while the battery's median over ten splits never
rejected; \(Q_1\) in its joint form 6 percent; \(Q_2\) 6 percent. Fok and
Paap's variance-difference form of \(Q_1\) rejected 4 percent of the time, but
a separate 1,000-replication check of the same design showed that apparent
calibration to be accidental: its covariance difference was never positive semidefinite, 44
percent of its statistics were negative, and it rejected 3.3, 4.2, and 4.9
percent of the time at the 1, 5, and 10 percent levels, while the joint form
that `fp_tests()` uses by default rejected 1.5, 6.2, and 11.2 percent. An
indefinite covariance difference can give a statistic of either sign: every
form refers \(\max(\text{statistic}, 0)\) to the chi-square, so a negative
statistic has a p-value of 1. The practical recommendations that follow are:

* read the difference form of Hausman-McFadden as descriptive and rely on its
  joint form, a single Small-Hsiao split with its seed fixed in advance
  (`small_hsiao(m, seed = )`; the median over several splits, which
  `iia_tests()` reports, is descriptive: in the size study it rejected in none
  of 300 samples), \(Q_1\) in its joint form, and \(Q_2\);
* calibrate any of them with the parametric bootstrap when the decision matters
  (below);
* remember that these are tests of IIA against unspecified alternatives; the
  score test against a nested logit (below) and the likelihood-ratio tests
  against the fitted nested logit, HEV, or random-intercept model (vignette
  *Beyond IIA*) are the sharper instruments when the alternative is known.

## Parametric bootstrap calibration

`boot_pvalue()` takes a test function whose null hypothesis is the fitted
model (the IIA tests, `nested_lm_test()`, `captivity_test()`) and returns a
data frame with a `statistic` column, with its arguments, simulates `B` data
sets from the fitted model, refits, recomputes the statistic, and reports the bootstrap p-value
\((1 + k) / (B' + 1)\), with \(B'\) the replicates that succeeded and \(k\) the
number of their statistics at least as large as the observed one, next to the
asymptotic p-value. Replicates whose
refit fails or loses an alternative are dropped and counted (`n_failed`);
replicates whose refit raises a warning (diverging coefficients, a boundary, an
ill-conditioned information matrix) are kept and counted (`n_flagged`, with the
warnings tabulated in the attribute `flag_reasons`); the method line reports
each count that is not zero. It is the remedy for a test whose
finite-sample distribution is in doubt. Here it calibrates the two Fok-Paap
tests, the rows of the battery that reject:

```{r boot}
set.seed(2)
bf <- boot_pvalue(m, fp_tests, B = 399)
bf
attr(bf, "flag_reasons")
attr(bf, "p.boot_unflagged")
```

Calibrated, both Fok-Paap tests still reject. Two of the 399 replicate
statistics reach the observed \(Q_1\), so its `p.boot` is 0.0075. The 47
flagged replicates are simulated samples in which the nonresponse equation
separates on `voted2004`, the same mechanism as in the Small-Hsiao splits
above: its intercept falls and its `voted2004` coefficient rises without
bound, their sum staying finite. Along that one direction neither estimator
has an estimate, so \(Q_1\) leaves it out and compares the two estimators
on the other 23, and \(Q_2\) is not computed in those samples (`?fp_tests`).
Five of the other 352 replicate statistics reach the observed \(Q_2\), and
its `p.boot` is 0.017. The observed data do not separate, and over the
unflagged replicates the two p-values are 0.0085 and 0.017.

## The score test against a nested logit

McFadden's (1987) Lagrange-multiplier test asks whether the inclusive-value
parameters of a specified nest structure differ from 1 (the multinomial logit)
without fitting the nested model; `nested_lm_test()` computes it from the
multinomial-logit scores (the outer-product information by default, the form
of McFadden's regression test). Here the nests group the two forms of
non-participation against the two forms of voting.

```{r nlm}
nests <- list(out = c("abstain", "nonresponse"), vote = c("democrat", "republican_other"))
nested_lm_test(m, nests)
```

## Further specification tests

`combine_test()` asks whether two alternatives can be pooled (Cramer and
Ridder 1991): the Wald form tests the equality of their slope coefficients
(the constants stay free), the likelihood-ratio form compares the pooled fit
with a binomial split term.
`term_test()` tests every covariate jointly across equations (Wald or LR).
`hl_test()` is the Hosmer-Lemeshow-type goodness-of-fit test for multinomial
models of Fagerland, Hosmer, and Bofin (2008), with the observed and expected
counts by decile of risk.

```{r spec}
combine_test(m, c("democrat", "republican_other"))
term_test(m)
hl_test(m)
```

On these data the Hosmer-Lemeshow-type test rejects clearly: the fitted
probabilities of the multinomial logit are miscalibrated in some deciles of
risk, a sign of misspecification that the IIA tests above do not locate.

The IIA tests, the score test, `combine_test()`, and `term_test()` return data
frames of class `"iia_test"` with a `statistic` column, so their results can be
bound and filtered. `boot_pvalue()` calibrates those whose null is the fitted
model; `term_test()` and `combine_test()` test a restriction of it and are
refused (for a term, fit the model without it and use `boot_lr_test()`).
`hl_test()` returns a list
with the observed and expected tables, and `iia_tests()` a data frame of class
`"iia_battery"`.

## References

Cramer, J.S. and Ridder, G. (1991). Pooling states in the multinomial logit
model. *Journal of Econometrics*, 47, 267-272.

Fagerland, M.W., Hosmer, D.W. and Bofin, A.M. (2008). Multinomial
goodness-of-fit tests for logistic regression models. *Statistics in
Medicine*, 27, 4238-4253.

Fok, D. and Paap, R. (2025). New misspecification tests for multinomial logit
models. *Journal of Choice Modelling*, 54, 100531.
doi:10.1016/j.jocm.2024.100531.

Hausman, J.A. and McFadden, D. (1984). Specification tests for the multinomial
logit model. *Econometrica*, 52, 1219-1240.

McFadden, D. (1987). Regression-based specification tests for the multinomial
logit model. *Journal of Econometrics*, 34, 63-82.

Small, K.A. and Hsiao, C. (1985). Multinomial logit specification tests.
*International Economic Review*, 26, 619-627.
